{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310557,"nonce":"w5JsCSAEuqc2DfhQozfEV9jFnW4=","title":"FIXED: rayzor_tensor_clone now ALWAYS compacts to contiguous. Result is a fresh owns_data=true tensor with canonical row-major strides and ...","message":"**Status: FIXED** (runtime/src/tensor.rs `rayzor_tensor_clone`).\n\n\nAfter commit d2e6d27 `rayzor_tensor_clone` unconditionally deep-copied, but to preserve view semantics it set `owns_data=true` on the new wrapper while *inheriting the non-contiguous strides of the parent view* and sizing the buffer to `(Σ(shape[i]-1)·strides[i] + 1)·elem_size` rather than `numel·elem_size`.\n\nReal consequence (the footgun): `rayzor_tensor_free` routes `owns_data=true` tensors into the tensor pool. The pool's `(dtype, shape)` key cannot distinguish a clone-of-view (non-contiguous strides, wrong byte count) from a canonically-contiguous owning tensor of the same shape. A later `alloc_tensor` of the same `(dtype, shape)` would pop the clone-of-view wrapper and hand the caller a \"contiguous\" tensor backed by view strides and a buffer of the wrong size — silently corrupting any stride-1 fast-path that gates on `owns_data`.\n\n\n`rayzor_tensor_clone` now always produces a contiguous owning tensor:\n\n1. Compute canonical row-major strides from `src.shape` (via `RayzorTensor::compute_strides`).\n2. Allocate fresh data buffer of exactly `numel * dtype_size(src.dtype)` bytes.\n3. If `src.is_contiguous()` → single `memcpy` of `numel * elem_size`.\n4. Else → strided gather walking `src` by its strides, copying `elem_size` bytes per logical element into the dest's row-major order. Dtype-agnostic byte-block copy handles quantised dtypes (Q4_K_M, Q8_0, …) the same way.\n5. Allocate fresh shape array (copied from src) and fresh strides array seeded with the *canonical* row-major strides, NOT inherited from src.\n6. `owns_data=true` on the new wrapper.\n\nResult: `owns_data == true` is now a true contiguity invariant runtime-wide. Pool-admit is always safe.\n\n\n`RayzorTensor::owns_data`: now states `owns_data=true` implies canonical row-major strides AND `data_bytes == numel * dtype_size(dtype)`. View producers (`permute`, `slice`, `transpose*`, `reshape`-aliased) set `owns_data=false` and may carry arbitrary strides; everyone else upholds the contiguity invariant.\n\n`RayzorTensor::is_contiguous`: the warning that \"owns_data is NOT a contiguity proxy after d2e6d27\" is removed; the helper still exists for fast-paths that take possibly-view inputs (`!owns_data` tensors can be non-contiguous).\n\n\n- `tensor::tests::tensor_clone_of_permuted_view_compacts_to_contiguous` — builds a 2×3 F32 tensor, permutes to a [3,2] view with strides [1,3], clones, asserts: `owns_data=true`, `is_contiguous()`, strides == [2,1] (canonical, not inherited), element-by-element value match between view and clone under permuted indexing, mutating the clone does not touch the source.\n- `tensor::tests::tensor_clone_of_contiguous_is_independent` — fast-path memcpy regression: clone of an already-contiguous tensor produces a fresh, value-equal, independently-mutable owning tensor.\n\nBoth pass under `cargo test --release -p rayzor-runtime --lib tensor_clone`.\n\n\nThe original memory listed kernels that \"should\" gate on `is_contiguous` rather than `owns_data`: `matmul`/`qmatmul` stride-1 shortcuts, any future `tensor_copy_contiguous` ABI, addInto fused kernel. Those should still call `is_contiguous` for inputs that may be views (`!owns_data`) — the fix here only closes the safety hole on the *owning* side. View-input fast-paths still need explicit stride checks.\n\nRelated: [[project-move-clone-enforced]] (commit d2e6d27 introduced the original behaviour).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_clone_view_passthrough_invariant` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}